WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.630
A fake AI app went round our school group.

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.740
It was called “SuperTutor AI free”, and it

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.060
promised a better chatbot, offline.

4
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.200
It stole the passwords

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.740
saved on three phones.

6
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.670
Today: how AI models and apps reach you,

7
00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.490
and how to check one before you trust it.

8
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.810
Every AI tool is built from parts

9
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.350
made by others: training data, a

10
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.750
model file, code and an app.

11
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.930
That chain of parts is the supply chain.

12
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.430
Any link can be faked, or

13
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.730
changed on the way to you.

14
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.290
Three things go wrong.

15
00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.050
A fake copy pretends to be the real model.

16
00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.030
A real file is changed on the way,

17
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.550
by one byte or a million.

18
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.380
And some model files can run hidden

19
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.160
code the moment you load them.

20
00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.800
The first check is a fingerprint.

21
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.840
SHA-256 turns any file into

22
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.600
sixty-four hex characters.

23
00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.990
Change one byte, and about half of the

24
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.270
underlying bits flip, so almost every

25
00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.370
character looks different.

26
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.950
If yours matches the one the real

27
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.670
publisher posted, the bytes are the same.

28
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.260
In Python it takes three lines.

29
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.040
Hash the file's bytes, turn the

30
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.220
result into hex, and compare it

31
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.180
with the published fingerprint.

32
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.480
Equal means unchanged.

33
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:28.570
Quick check!

34
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.280
You change one letter inside a model file.

35
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.930
What happens to its fingerprint?

36
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.300
About half of its bits flip, so

37
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.380
even a tiny edit can't hide.

38
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.950
But a fingerprint only proves

39
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.450
the bytes didn't change.

40
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.180
It can't tell you who made them.

41
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.340
For that, you need a signature.

42
00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.840
The publisher signs the file with a

43
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.120
private key, and anyone can check

44
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.400
it with their public key.

45
00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.490
An impostor can post a fake with its

46
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.070
own fingerprint, but they can't sign

47
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.070
as the real publisher.

48
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.080
So build three habits.

49
00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.210
Download only from the official source.

50
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.620
Check the fingerprint and the

51
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.660
signature before you run anything.

52
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.680
And keep a list of the models you use:

53
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.760
name, version, where from, fingerprint.

54
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.870
In the app, leave the file

55
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.750
genuine: both checks are green.

56
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.700
Change one byte: about half its bits flip.

57
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.170
Then try the impostor: the bytes look

58
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.010
fine, but the signature fails.

59
00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.190
Your turn.

60
00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.050
A model's fingerprint matches the

61
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.470
one posted right next to it, on a

62
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:45.250
site you don't know.

63
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.200
Can you trust it?

64
00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.770
Not yet: whoever posted the file

65
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.410
posted that fingerprint too.

66
00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.750
Remember: any link in the

67
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.930
chain can be faked.

68
00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.350
A fingerprint proves the same bytes, and

69
00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.130
a signature proves who made them.

70
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.940
Use official sources, check

71
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.100
both, and keep a list.

72
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.840
Try it tomorrow: with the owner, look

73
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.900
at the AI apps on a family phone.

74
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.780
Write where each came from: the

75
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.320
store, a shared file or a website.

76
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.490
Remove what you can't check,

77
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.270
and start your model list.
